WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.394 --> 00:00:05.280
On March 5th, 2022, Russian gymnast Ivan Kuliak
stepped on the podium

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:09.760
at the International Gymnastics&nbsp;
Federation World Cup in Doha, Qatar.

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:12.000
He won the bronze medal in the parallel bars.

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:16.960
Next to him was Illia Kovtun, the&nbsp;
gold medal winner from Ukraine

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:21.090
and silver medalist Milad&nbsp;
Karimi, representing Kazahstan.

00:00:21.090 --> 00:00:23.613
The situation was already tense.

00:00:23.613 --> 00:00:27.632
Russia’s invasion of Ukraine had&nbsp;
begun just nine days earlier.

00:00:27.632 --> 00:00:33.712
And Kuliak decided to show his support for the&nbsp;
war, by attaching this symbol to his uniform:

00:00:33.712 --> 00:00:37.229
three pieces of white tape, forming the letter Z.

00:00:37.229 --> 00:00:41.408
By that time, the Z was&nbsp;
becoming an obsession in Russia.

00:00:41.408 --> 00:00:43.840
And a controversial symbol worldwide

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:47.072
as the new way to signal approval for the war

00:00:47.072 --> 00:00:50.592
and loyalty to Russian President Vladimir Putin.

00:00:50.592 --> 00:00:56.493
Since the invasion began in late February, the Z&nbsp;
has shown up on billboards in St. Petersburg...

00:00:56.493 --> 00:00:58.732
T-shirt stands in Moscow...

00:00:58.732 --> 00:01:00.000
Auto rallys...

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:03.638
And flash mobs created for social media.

00:01:03.638 --> 00:01:08.480
There’s even this photo – posted on the&nbsp;
website of a children’s cancer hospice

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:13.605
showing sick children organized into&nbsp;
the shape, outside, in the snow.

00:01:13.605 --> 00:01:18.272
The letter has been used to vandalize&nbsp;
the homes of Russians who oppose the war

00:01:18.272 --> 00:01:22.394
and is now banned from public&nbsp;
display in several countries.

00:01:22.394 --> 00:01:27.265
So how did this symbol become shorthand&nbsp;
for supporting Russian aggression?

00:01:30.186 --> 00:01:34.080
The letter Z was first&nbsp;
spotted in late February 2022

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:37.728
on Russian military equipment&nbsp;
gathering at Ukraine’s border.

00:01:37.728 --> 00:01:42.228
Along with other painted&nbsp;
white markings, like V and O.

00:01:42.228 --> 00:01:45.291
At first, what these symbols meant was unclear

00:01:45.291 --> 00:01:50.560
Z isn’t part of the Cyrillic Alphabet –
the writing system used in Russia and Ukraine.

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:54.112
The Z sound in Cyrillic is&nbsp;
written sort of like a 3.

00:01:54.112 --> 00:01:57.040
And “V” looks like the Latin letter “B.”

00:01:57.040 --> 00:02:02.080
But once the invasion began, most analysts&nbsp;
agreed the markings were for tactical purposes

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:04.893
likely to prevent confusion on the battlefield.

00:02:04.893 --> 00:02:09.199
And potentially denoting where&nbsp;
specific forces were attacking from.

00:02:09.199 --> 00:02:12.331
The Russian military didn’t&nbsp;
explain what the markings meant.

00:02:12.331 --> 00:02:15.490
Until intrigue around them grew online

00:02:15.490 --> 00:02:20.480
and people in Russia saw the chance&nbsp;
to use the Z as a tool for propaganda.

00:02:21.565 --> 00:02:25.791
One of the most common ways that the&nbsp;letter Z
is being given meaning in&nbsp;Russian

00:02:25.791 --> 00:02:30.592
is by latinizing it in the&nbsp;Russian word 
"Za" which means for.

00:02:30.592 --> 00:02:32.880
And in incorporating it into slogans like

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:33.942
[speaking in Russian] For victory.

00:02:33.942 --> 00:02:35.000
[speaking in Russian] For the president.

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:35.840
[speaking in Russian] For the boys.

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:38.074
So there was an attempt to fill it.

00:02:38.074 --> 00:02:39.533
That's Aglaya Snetkov.

00:02:39.533 --> 00:02:45.232
She's a lecturer on Russian foreign, security,&nbsp;
and domestic policy at University College London.

00:02:45.232 --> 00:02:50.567
You increasingly have pictures&nbsp;
which are so absurdly staged.

00:02:50.567 --> 00:02:52.912
It's obviously fake, but&nbsp;it sort of 
doesn't matter.

00:02:52.912 --> 00:02:55.866
So the pictures of the kids, for&nbsp;
example, who can't even read or write

00:02:55.866 --> 00:02:59.760
they obviously have no idea what the Z&nbsp;
they're holding means or anything like that.

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:03.638
But it sort of almost doesn't matter&nbsp;
so long as you throw it out there.

00:03:03.638 --> 00:03:08.327
Click on these slogans’ hashtags,&nbsp;
like “for russia” and “for peace”

00:03:08.327 --> 00:03:11.920
and you’ll find tens of thousands&nbsp;
of posts on Instagram alone.

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:15.589
Many of them originating from&nbsp;
the Russian Ministry of Defense.

00:03:15.589 --> 00:03:19.194
The political technologists in Russia, in Kremlin,

00:03:19.194 --> 00:03:21.360
and in and around the Ministry of Defense

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:23.680
have decided to jump on the opportunity

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:27.687
and reinfuse the meaning of&nbsp;
those obscure symbols.

00:03:27.687 --> 00:03:32.290
Kiril Avramov teaches classes on Russian&nbsp;
and Soviet symbolism and propaganda&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.290 --> 00:03:34.487
at the University of Texas, Austin.

00:03:34.487 --> 00:03:38.013
Avramov told me the point of&nbsp;
these memes is to turn the Z&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.013 --> 00:03:41.146
and sometimes the V into something cool.

00:03:41.146 --> 00:03:42.575
He translated a few of them.

00:03:43.413 --> 00:03:46.938
Is like street slang for "for the boys".

00:03:50.173 --> 00:03:56.095
"Power is in truth." Which is actually&nbsp;
a quote from a post-Soviet blockbuster.

00:03:57.312 --> 00:04:03.360
They're picking already familiar slogans to&nbsp;
post-Soviet Russians and putting into those visuals

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:05.915
and then recreating those in flash mobs... &nbsp;

00:04:05.915 --> 00:04:06.770
graffiti...

00:04:06.770 --> 00:04:09.603
and all the way to the&nbsp;
presidential administration.

00:04:11.029 --> 00:04:15.269
One strategy for turning Z into&nbsp;
a patriotic propaganda icon

00:04:15.269 --> 00:04:18.284
has been connecting it to&nbsp;
Russian glory of the past

00:04:18.284 --> 00:04:21.706
in particular, the Soviet&nbsp;
victory in World War II.
 &nbsp;

00:04:21.706 --> 00:04:27.440
The Soviet Union suffered devastating losses&nbsp;
defending itself against invasion by Nazi Germany.

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:29.600
But they were ultimately victorious,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:34.000
with the Red Army fighting its way&nbsp;
back to Berlin and defeating Hitler,

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:37.527
establishing the Soviet&nbsp;
Union as a world superpower.

00:04:37.527 --> 00:04:43.760
The “Great Patriotic War” is still a potent&nbsp;
symbol of heroism and glory in Russia.

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:50.480
And is honored each year on May 9th, or “Victory&nbsp;
Day” - the anniversary of Germany’s capitulation.

00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:54.210
With massive demonstrations&nbsp;
of modern military might

00:04:54.210 --> 00:04:57.509
and remembrance of ancestors&nbsp;
who were killed in the war.

00:04:57.509 --> 00:05:00.800
The historical trauma is immense in Soviet times.

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:05.000
There was a huge sacrifice on&nbsp;
the part of the Soviet people.

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:07.847
And those are some highly symbolic events.

00:05:07.847 --> 00:05:13.680
This ever-present historical awareness makes&nbsp;
World War II imagery a direct link to patriotism,

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:18.167
and presents the opportunity to&nbsp;
connect the nostalgia of Soviet heroism&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.167 --> 00:05:21.156
with Russia’s current attacks on Ukraine.

00:05:21.156 --> 00:05:24.720
Take this meme posted to the Russian&nbsp;
Ministry of Defense’s Instagram

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:30.720
on March 4, 2022, which as of&nbsp;
early April has over 21,000 likes.

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:36.400
It shows a “Z” superimposed over a black and&nbsp;
white photo of Soviet soldiers on one side...

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:40.887
and a photo of modern Russian soldiers&nbsp;
looking back on the other side.

00:05:40.887 --> 00:05:43.004
The hashtag says “heroes".

00:05:43.004 --> 00:05:49.040
The photo on the left is a famous image from&nbsp;
the original Moscow Victory Parade of 1945&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:51.010
celebrating Germany’s surrender.

00:05:51.010 --> 00:05:55.142
It’s still the largest parade&nbsp;ever held 
in Moscow’s Red Square 

00:05:55.142 --> 00:05:58.290
organized&nbsp;by Soviet dictator Joseph Stalin.

00:05:58.290 --> 00:06:04.320
The image shows Soviet soldiers carrying captured&nbsp;
Nazi flags and banners before destroying them

00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:09.712
and has been used in Soviet and post-Soviet&nbsp;
patriotic propaganda ever since.

00:06:09.712 --> 00:06:14.031
So every Russian and Soviet person&nbsp;
would recognize that immediately.

00:06:14.031 --> 00:06:18.186
The “Z” is paired with the slogan&nbsp;
“za pobedu” - “for victory”

00:06:18.186 --> 00:06:20.800
and is styled in black and orange stripes.

00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:25.632
Which, other than plain white, is the&nbsp;
most common way the Z is displayed.

00:06:25.632 --> 00:06:29.546
The pattern represents the color&nbsp;
scheme of the Ribbon of St. George

00:06:29.546 --> 00:06:32.050
one of Putin’s favorite propaganda symbols.

00:06:32.050 --> 00:06:35.600
Its origins trace back through&nbsp;
hundreds of years of Russian history

00:06:35.600 --> 00:06:38.389
as a symbol of military glory.

00:06:38.389 --> 00:06:42.149
At the end of World War II, Stalin&nbsp;
used the ribbon on a medal awarded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.149 --> 00:06:45.767
to all Soviets who served in&nbsp;
the war – including civilians.

00:06:45.767 --> 00:06:50.080
The ribbon, which everyone knows is, you&nbsp;
know, if you see it it's the Russian military&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:53.140
might and its a signifier that you're Russian.

00:06:53.140 --> 00:06:57.373
The ribbon of St. George is now handed&nbsp;
out every year ahead of Victory Day

00:06:57.373 --> 00:07:00.149
and is a central emblem of the celebrations.

00:07:00.149 --> 00:07:05.280
Invoking the memory of glorious past in&nbsp;
order to play this propaganda trick, right?

00:07:05.280 --> 00:07:11.006
Equating this war with this one, in&nbsp;
terms of justness and legitimacy.
 &nbsp;

00:07:12.007 --> 00:07:15.712
Beyond emerging as the new&nbsp;
mark of loyalty to Putin

00:07:15.712 --> 00:07:18.333
the Z doesn’t hold any individual meaning.

00:07:18.333 --> 00:07:21.362
And that seems to be part&nbsp;
of the strategy behind it.

00:07:21.362 --> 00:07:24.880
And that, I think, shows the way&nbsp;
in which Putin's propaganda works,

00:07:24.880 --> 00:07:27.920
He throws things out there, sees&nbsp;
what lands, and then goes with that.

00:07:27.920 --> 00:07:31.066
The symbol just became&nbsp;
recognizable, the explanations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.066 --> 00:07:36.524
which I call "post rationalization" is that this&nbsp;
is all planned, what the Zs and the Vs stand for.

00:07:36.524 --> 00:07:39.506
For victory. For Zelenskyy. Or you name it.

00:07:39.506 --> 00:07:41.170
The advancement of so many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.170 --> 00:07:45.000
alternative explanations actually adds energy 
to the state propaganda.

00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:48.074
Whether or not any of these&nbsp;
explanations end up sticking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.074 --> 00:07:51.488
the Z’s evolution
from tanks and trucks...

00:07:51.488 --> 00:07:52.530
to memes...

00:07:52.530 --> 00:07:54.793
then flash mobs around Russia...

00:07:54.793 --> 00:07:57.968
to pro-Putin demonstrations around the world...

00:07:57.968 --> 00:08:04.480
shows that as a symbol’s visibility grows,&nbsp;
so does its power as a tool for propaganda.

